\title{Functional Fibres, Experiential Gaps, and Labelled Incompleteness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a geometric model of the distinction between functional description and a Boolean experiential coordinate. A system is represented by a state space $X$, a behavioral space $B$, an observation map $F:X\to B$, and an experience map $E:X\to\{0,1\}$. Functional identity means equality under $F$, while an oriented zombie pair is an aware state and an experientially void state in one fibre of $F$. In the split model $X=B\times\{0,1\}$, the experiential coordinate admits a fibre-preserving involution. Every aware state has a unique void twin, the space of oriented gaps is naturally bijective with $B$, and any pseudometric pulled back from $B$ assigns zero functional distance to every twin pair. For arbitrary $X$, experience factors through functional observation exactly when it is constant on every fibre. A constant-aware model proves that functional organization alone cannot imply zombie existence. Finally, fixing an indexed consistency sentence independent in both polarities in a standard provability system, we construct a labelled incompleteness-gap space. It too is classified by $B$, yielding a label-preserving isomorphism between the two gap spaces. The result compares explicit structures and does not identify phenomenology with syntax.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Functional descriptions concern observable outputs, transitions, reports, dispositions, and responses. The philosophical hard problem asks whether such descriptions determine what a system experiences. We do not attempt to settle that metaphysical question. Instead, we isolate its information geometry.

A map from total states to behavioral profiles may identify many states. The inverse image of a profile is a fibre, and a quantity varying within a fibre cannot be recovered from the profile alone. This distinguishes three claims: functional observation may omit information; experience may vary among functionally identical states; and every aware state may possess a unique void counterpart. The first is about compression, the second requires a variation premise, and the third requires additional global structure.

The product $B\times\{0,1\}$ provides a transparent model. Projection forgets the experiential bit, while Boolean negation supplies a canonical involution in each fibre. We establish unique twins, classify all oriented gaps, and identify the exact criterion for reconstructing experience from behavior.

A related architecture occurs in incompleteness. Fix a standard provability system $S$ and, for each index $i$, a consistency sentence $C_i$ for which neither $C_i$ nor $\neg C_i$ is provable. Pairing this fixed logical gap with a freely varying behavioral label again produces exactly one gap over every $b\in B$. The two spaces are therefore isomorphic over $B$. This is an objectwise structural bridge, not a semantic identification.

\section{Functional observation and experience}

\begin{definition}[Functional observation and fibres]
Let $X$ be a set of total states and $B$ a set of behavioral profiles. A \emph{functional observation} is a map $F:X\to B$. The fibre over $b\in B$ is
\[
F^{-1}(b)=\{x\in X:F(x)=b\}.
\]
States $x,y\in X$ are \emph{functionally identical} when $F(x)=F(y)$.
\end{definition}

Functional identity is an equivalence relation because equality in $B$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.

\begin{definition}[Experience and oriented zombie pairs]
An \emph{experience observable} is a map $E:X\to\{0,1\}$, where $1$ denotes awareness and $0$ experiential absence. An \emph{oriented zombie pair} is an ordered pair $(x,z)$ such that
\[
F(x)=F(z),\qquad E(x)=1,\qquad E(z)=0.
\]
\end{definition}

Orientation records the aware endpoint first. Existence of such a pair is equivalent to $E$ taking both Boolean values on at least one fibre: either a pair witnesses the variation, or aware and void members of a varying fibre form a pair.

\section{The split model}

Let
\[
X_B=B\times\{0,1\},\qquad F_B(b,q)=b,\qquad E_B(b,q)=q.
\]
Every fibre is the pair $\{(b,0),(b,1)\}$. This is a global splitting of total state into behavioral and experiential coordinates.

\begin{definition}[Qualia flip]
Define $Q:X_B\to X_B$ by
\[
Q(b,q)=(b,1-q).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre-preserving qualia involution]
For every $(b,q)\in B\times\{0,1\}$,
\[
F_B(Q(b,q))=F_B(b,q),\qquad Q(Q(b,q))=(b,q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The operation leaves the first coordinate $b$ unchanged, proving fibre preservation. Boolean negation is involutive, since $1-(1-q)=q$ for $q\in\{0,1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique zombie twin]
Every aware state $x=(b,1)$ in the split model has exactly one experientially void, functionally identical state. It is
\[
z=(b,0)=Q(x).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The proposed $z$ has the same behavioral coordinate and experiential value $0$. If $z'=(b',q')$ is any other candidate, functional identity forces $b'=b$ and voidness forces $q'=0$. Hence $z'=z$.
\end{proof}

The theorem is conditional on the split product. General fibres can have no void member, many void members, or richer experiential structure.

\section{Classification and metric geometry}

\begin{definition}[Experiential-gap space]
The oriented experiential-gap space $\ExpGap(B)$ consists of all ordered pairs of split states whose first endpoint is aware, whose second is void, and whose behavioral profiles agree.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Experiential gap classification]
There is a bijection
\[
\Phi_B:\ExpGap(B)\longrightarrow B
\]
that sends a gap to its common behavioral profile. Its inverse is
\[
\Phi_B^{-1}(b)=((b,1),(b,0)).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The experiential requirements force the Boolean coordinates to be $1$ and $0$, while functional identity forces equality of behavioral coordinates. Thus every gap has the unique displayed form. Extracting and reinserting $b$ are inverse operations.
\end{proof}

Consequently, $B$ is the moduli space of oriented split-model gaps. There is one canonical gap over each profile.

Suppose now that $B$ has a pseudometric $d_B$. For an arbitrary observation map $F:X\to B$, define the pulled-back functional pseudodistance
\[
d_F(x,y)=d_B(F(x),F(y)).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Zero functional distance]
If $(x,z)$ is an oriented zombie pair, then
\[
d_F(x,z)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $F(x)=F(z)$,
\[
d_F(x,z)=d_B(F(x),F(z))=d_B(F(x),F(x))=0.
\]
\end{proof}

The theorem concerns a metric derived solely from behavior. It does not claim that every metric on total states must assign distance zero. Rather, it states that a representation cannot metrically detect what it has identified.

\section{The reconstruction boundary}

Write $R=F(X)\subseteq B$ for the realized behavioral profiles.

\begin{definition}[Functional reconstruction]
Experience is \emph{functionally reconstructible} if there exists a map $e:R\to\{0,1\}$ such that
\[
E(x)=e(F(x))
\]
for every $x\in X$.
\end{definition}

The domain $R$ avoids assigning arbitrary values to unrealized profiles.

\begin{theorem}[Fibre-constancy criterion]
Experience is functionally reconstructible if and only if it is constant on every functional fibre. Explicitly,
\[
\exists e:R\to\{0,1\}\;\forall x\in X,\ E(x)=e(F(x))
\]
holds if and only if
\[
\forall x,y\in X,\quad F(x)=F(y)\Longrightarrow E(x)=E(y).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $E=e\circ F$ and $F(x)=F(y)$, then
\[
E(x)=e(F(x))=e(F(y))=E(y).
\]
Conversely, assume fibre constancy. For each $b\in R$, choose a representative $x_b$ satisfying $F(x_b)=b$ and set $e(b)=E(x_b)$. A different representative lies in the same fibre and therefore has the same experiential value, so $e$ is well defined. Taking $x$ itself as a representative of $F(x)$ yields $e(F(x))=E(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Zombie obstruction]
If an oriented zombie pair exists, experience is not functionally reconstructible.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The pair has one functional profile but two experiential values, contradicting fibre constancy.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Reconstruction excludes zombies]
If experience is functionally reconstructible, no oriented zombie pair exists.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Reconstruction implies fibre constancy, whereas a zombie pair violates it.
\end{proof}

For nonempty $B$, split experience is not reconstructible from split behavior because every fibre contains both experiential values.

\section{The indispensable hypothesis}

Non-injectivity of $F$ does not imply that experience varies on its fibres. A particularly simple countermodel makes this exact.

\begin{theorem}[Functionalism-alone countermodel]
Let $X,B$ be arbitrary sets, let $F:X\to B$ be any observation map, and define constant awareness by $E_{\top}(x)=1$ for every $x\in X$. Then no state has an experientially void functional twin.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A void twin $z$ would require $E_{\top}(z)=0$, while the definition gives $E_{\top}(z)=1$. This contradiction is independent of $F$.
\end{proof}

Thus functional organization alone cannot establish zombie existence. The correct existence claim must assume experiential variation in a fibre. The product model makes a stronger assumption: every fibre is globally and uniquely split into one aware and one void state.

\section{Labelled incompleteness gaps}

Fix a standard provability system $S$. For every natural number $i$, let $C_i$ be a designated consistency sentence satisfying the two-sided independence conditions
\[
S\nvdash C_i,
\qquad
S\nvdash\neg C_i.
\]
These conditions are the logical input to the construction.

\begin{definition}[Indexed labelled incompleteness gap]
For a label space $B$ and index $i$, the space $\IncGap(B,i)$ consists of pairs $(b,A)$ with $b\in B$, $A=C_i$, and the fixed certificate that $S\nvdash A$ and $S\nvdash\neg A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Indexed incompleteness gap classification]
For every $B$ and $i$, projection to the label defines a bijection
\[
\Psi_{B,i}:\IncGap(B,i)\longrightarrow B.
\]
Its inverse maps $b$ to $(b,C_i)$ equipped with the two-sided independence certificate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The sentence component is required to equal $C_i$, and the certificate is fixed. Hence $b$ is the only variable datum. Projection and reinsertion are mutual inverses.
\end{proof}

\section{The gap isomorphism}

Both gap spaces have now been classified by $B$. Composing the first classification with the inverse of the second gives the desired bridge.

\begin{theorem}[Experiential--incompleteness gap isomorphism]
For every behavioral space $B$ and index $i$, there is a bijection
\[
\Theta_{B,i}:\ExpGap(B)\longrightarrow\IncGap(B,i)
\]
given explicitly by
\[
\Theta_{B,i}(((b,1),(b,0)))=(b,C_i),
\]
where the image carries the fixed certificate that neither $C_i$ nor $\neg C_i$ is provable in $S$. Its inverse sends $(b,C_i)$ to $((b,1),(b,0))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $\Theta_{B,i}=\Psi_{B,i}^{-1}\circ\Phi_B$. It is a composition of bijections and hence a bijection. The explicit formula follows from extracting the shared label $b$ and reconstructing the indexed logical gap over that same label.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Label preservation]
The isomorphism preserves the behavioral label and assigns the designated sentence:
\[
\Theta_{B,i}(((b,1),(b,0)))=(b,C_i).
\]
Thus the first coordinate of the image is $b$ and its sentence coordinate is $C_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
This follows directly from the construction through the common classifier $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Interpretive scope]
The theorem is an isomorphism between explicit labelled structures. It does not identify experience with formal syntax, nor does it claim that incompleteness explains consciousness. The common feature is exactly one fixed, oriented or two-sided gap over each visible label.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Unique twin construction}

Given an aware split state $(b,1)$, output $(b,0)$. Correctness is Theorem~3.2: the output preserves $b$, is void, and is unique. If $b$ is held by immutable reference, the operation takes $O(1)$ time and space; if a length-$n$ label must be copied, it takes $O(n)$ time and output space.

\subsection{Gap encoding and decoding}

To encode $((b,1),(b,0))$, return $b$. To decode $b$, construct that pair. Theorem~4.2 shows that these operations are inverse. Enumerating gaps over $n$ labels takes $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ output space, without a quadratic search through pairs of states.

\subsection{Finite fibre-constancy test}

For finite data $(x,F(x),E(x))$, maintain a dictionary from behavioral profiles to the first observed experiential value. If a profile later appears with a different value, return the conflicting pair as a certificate of non-reconstructibility. If the list exhausts a finite model and no conflict occurs, Theorem~5.2 proves reconstructibility on that model. With hashable profiles, expected running time is $O(n)$ and space is $O(k)$, where $k$ is the number of distinct profiles. A sorting implementation takes $O(n\log n)$ time.

\subsection{Bridge construction}

Given an experiential gap and index $i$, extract $b$ and return $(b,C_i)$ with the fixed independence certificate. The inverse extracts $b$ and forms the canonical aware--void pair. Computationally, the bridge transports the variable label while its logical component remains fixed.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Representation learning.}
A learned representation may collapse source states. A target attribute varying among collapsed states cannot be perfectly predicted from that representation. The fibre-constancy criterion is the exact noiseless test for perfect downstream prediction.

\paragraph{Measurement.}
An instrument induces an observation map. Distinct states at zero pulled-back distance need not be physically identical; they are indistinguishable to the instrument. Adding sensors refines fibres, while removing sensors coarsens them.

\paragraph{Privacy.}
A released representation may intentionally forget a sensitive Boolean attribute. The split model idealizes perfect hiding by placing both values over every public profile. Conversely, fibre constancy means the sensitive attribute is exactly reconstructible on the realized range.

\paragraph{Structural comparison.}
Two theories can have gap objects classified by the same parameter space without sharing an interpretation. A label-preserving bijection transfers indexing information while respecting that distinction.

\section{Discussion}

The product model is strong because its assumptions are strong. It separates behavior and experience globally. The qualia flip, uniqueness theorem, and exact classification all follow from that splitting. They are representation results rather than empirical claims.

Three conditions should be kept separate. Non-injectivity of $F$ says only that some total-state distinction is forgotten. Experiential fibre variation says that experience is among what is forgotten. A two-sheeted splitting says that every profile has exactly one state of each experiential polarity. Only the last condition gives the full canonical construction.

The incompleteness bridge is similarly controlled. Once $i$ is fixed, the sentence $C_i$ and its certificate are fixed, while $b$ varies. The shared classification therefore gives an exact bijection. Causal or ontological conclusions would require additional hypotheses not present here.

\section{Future directions}

The trivial product should be replaced by nontrivial finite coverings of a topological behavioral space. Local twins may exist without a continuous global twin operation; monodromy would then provide an obstruction to global splitting.

Exact functional equality should also admit a robust metric analogue. For compact state spaces and Lipschitz experiential observables, fixed experiential contrast together with small functional distance may force a lower bound on the error of every attempted functional reconstruction.

Boolean experience can be generalized to a finite complex or another structured space $Q$. Components, loops, and higher homotopy in experiential fibres could describe forms of variation invisible to functional projection.

The bridge presented here is objectwise. A categorical formulation should define morphisms between labelled functional quotients and between theories, then ask whether the gap correspondence is natural. Such naturality would distinguish a structural bridge from an accidental shared classifier.

Finally, fibre constancy guarantees extensional factorization, but computable reconstruction on effectively presented spaces may require effective access to representatives. This separates set-theoretic recoverability from algorithmic recoverability.

\section{Conclusion}

Functional observation partitions total states into fibres. Experience can be reconstructed from behavioral data exactly when it is constant on those fibres. In a split Boolean model, a fibre-preserving involution gives every aware state a unique void twin; the gap space is classified by $B$; and every pair has zero distance under a metric pulled back solely from behavior.

The constant-aware countermodel proves that functional organization alone does not imply zombie existence. Fibre variation or splitting must be supplied as a premise.

A labelled two-sided incompleteness construction is also classified by $B$, yielding a label-preserving isomorphism of gap spaces. The correspondence identifies a common mathematical architecture while preserving the distinction between phenomenology and syntax. What an observation map forgets lies in its fibres, and no function of its output can recover a quantity that varies within them.

